          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"colette-le-ble-en-herbe-2344cebfeaed45b2\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Vinca directly avows that she still loves Philippe. The temporal adverb « toujours » emphasizes the continuing truth of her emotional state, while « Malheureusement, ça n'y change rien » explicitly separates that state from forgiveness, reconciliation, or any renewed relational undertaking. Her gentle, controlled delivery supplies no independent evidence that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Vinca directly addresses Philippe: « Oh! Phil, je t'aime toujours. »\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal answers Philippe's fear that Vinca no longer loves him, but it is concessive rather than reassuring: she maintains that her love persists while insisting that it changes nothing about « nous ». The subsequent discussion of their former expectation of marriage and her wish to become a child again indicates a rupture or transformation that love alone cannot repair. Philippe partially receives her words as absolution, but the narration stresses both her sincere tone and her verbal reticence. These complications concern the consequences and interpretation of the avowal, not its core truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Oh! Phil, je t'aime toujours. Malheureusement, ça n'y change rien. »\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense statement, strengthened by « toujours », strongly supports T; the qualification denies that the avowal itself changes the relationship and therefore weighs against P.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Philippe asks whether his weakness is enough to make Vinca seem not to love him anymore, and her highlighted utterance answers that question.\",\"supports\":\"Frames the utterance as a direct answer about whether her loving state continues, supporting a truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Vinca later clarifies that nothing changes « À nous, Phil » and recalls their formerly anticipated marriage.\",\"supports\":\"Shows that continuing love is explicitly distinguished from reconciliation or renewal of their shared undertaking, supporting P=0.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration describes her speech as marked by « une douceur sibylline » and later by « tant de sincérité dans l'accent, tant de réticence dans les mots ».\",\"supports\":\"Supports a sincere but controlled and guarded avowal rather than an affect-driven verbal discharge; the initial « Oh! » alone does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit first-person love expression in Colette's Le blé en herbe.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical and bibliographic identification of the classified event.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as an avowal of continuing love. The surrounding relational rupture and Philippe's interpretation are adequately treated as context; they create no residual pressure for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
